},{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"discussion","text":"Discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/AstroTime\/addingDays(_:)","isActive":true},{"text":" adds to Universal Time and derives Terrestrial Time from the Delta","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"T model, so an offset across a point where the model’s pieces meet with a step","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(Espenak–Meeus steps back 0.088 seconds in January 1900) moves TT by ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"days"},{"text":" plus or","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"minus that step. This method adds ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"days"},{"text":" to both ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/AstroTime\/universalTime","isActive":true},{"text":" and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/AstroTime\/terrestrialTime","isActive":true},{"text":", so TT moves by exactly ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"days"},{"text":" and neither clock steps. Its UT","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"differs from the Delta T model’s UT for the new TT by Delta T’s change over ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"days"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"under 1e-7 of ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"days"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Use it for short offsets around a time, such as the samples of a numerical","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"derivative; use ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/AstroTime\/addingDays(_:)","isActive":true},{"text":" to step through civil time.","type":"text"}]}],"kin